    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d704">

                <pb facs="0809" n="721" xml:id="pg721"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. McCormick to Mr.
                            Hay.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Embassy</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">St.
                                Petersburg</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1904-05-14"><hi rendition="#italic">May 14,
                        1904</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 134.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to the Department’s cable
                    instruction of yesterday requesting that I telegraph the full names of certain
                    Japanese officers and men captured May 2 and 3, while taking part in the Port
                    Arthur blockading party, and also of those taken prisoners when the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Kinshu Maru</hi> was sunk, I have the honor to state
                    that I had already asked the ministry for foreign affairs to furnish me with the
                    full names of these officers and all of their fellow-prisoners, and that His
                    Excellency Count Lamsdorff had already caused a telegram to be sent to Admiral
                    Alexieff requesting that these names be telegraphed as soon as possible, and I
                    hope to receive them within a day or two.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">* * * * * * *</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert S.
                    McCormick.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
